From 9a3345b1241d2df727393b389a07f98c4869c329 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Craig Johnson <1545411+captainpacket@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 22 Sep 2026 18:24:55 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] api: do not bind a cross-node vxlan to the underlay device

A vxlan created with a lower device (VtepDevIndex, `dev eno1`) is capped
by the kernel at lower MTU - 50, forever (vxlan_change_mtu; the lower
device is resolved in the netns the vxlan was created in, so the cap
follows the wire into the pod). A NOS that owns the interface cannot
raise it. cEOS raises a routed port's kernel ethN to 10240 when it moves
the port into a VRF (macvlan passthru into ns-<vrf> at the IP MTU); on a
cross-node wire that fails EINVAL and the port never enters its VRF.

Measured 2026-09-22, user-skyforge-demo-estate-13-fu-798b2b0d, one lab,
one config shape:
  siteA-dc1-veos-wanleaf1 eth4: veth (same node), mtu 10240; ns-VRF_WWW
    holds eth4 macvlan passthru mtu 8950, 10.254.2.17/30.
  siteF-dc1-veos-wanleaf1 eth4/eth5: vxlan with a lower dev, mtu 8950;
    ns-VRF_WWW/ns-VRF_DB hold no eth4/eth5; EOS says "VRF_WWW" while
    `show vrf` lists neither port.

Create the wire UNBOUND when the path is proven: the kernel route to the
peer VTEP leaves via the underlay interface and that interface has an
IPv4 address. The source is then pinned (IFLA_VXLAN_LOCAL) to that
address -- the VTEP meshnet already advertises -- so the outer header is
what the bound form sent. The wire is still created at the requested
fabric MTU (0007); with none requested it gets lower MTU - 50 (what the
bound form defaulted to), and a request above that is clamped (what the
bound form's kernel did). Anything unproven (no route, a route via
another device, IPv6, multicast, no address) falls back to the upstream
bound form, logged with the reason.

Compatibility, so a rollout rebuilds nothing:
- Both forms emit identical packets; a wire with one bound and one
  unbound end works (proven in netns: DF ping at 8922 across a mixed
  pair, then through a cEOS-style macvlan passthru after raising the
  unbound end to 10240).
- Form is not part of sameVxLanSpec, so legacy bound wires are adopted.
- sameVxLanSpec treats the fabric MTU as a floor: a wire above it (the
  NOS raised it) matches; below it (stale 1450) still does not.
- configureLinkInNS (vxlan and veth adopt paths) raises to the fabric
  MTU but never lowers an MTU the device owner raised (mtuToSet).
- isConflictingHostVxLan counts an unbound (VtepDevIndex 0) occupant:
  the kernel's IPv4 VNI check ignores the lower device, so it collides.

Tests: pure decision/builder tests plus kernel tests gated on
KOKO_KERNEL_TESTS=1 under `unshare -rn`. Positive control: before this
change TestKernelUnboundWireAcceptsANOSRaisedMTU failed "invalid
argument" (vtepdev=2) and the conflict scan found 0 unbound occupants.
